---
import ProjectPage from "../../layouts/ProjectPage.astro";

const paper = "https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06352";
---

<ProjectPage
    title="Autonomous Blimp"
    description="Championship autonomy software for a lighter-than-air robot. The published design is arXiv:2309.06352. The site record is 152–0 on a 150 g airframe."
    lede="Championship autonomy software for a lighter-than-air robot. The published design is the 2023 scoring-agent paper."
    figureLabel="Fig. 02 — Autonomous Blimp"
    figureAlt="Schematic of a blimp envelope, thrusters, and a vision target."
    plate="blimp"
    figureNote="Plate"
    primary={{ href: paper, label: "Read the paper" }}
    secondary={{ href: "#how-it-works", label: "How it works" }}
    aboutTitle="The competition agent."
    sectionId="how-it-works"
    sectionTitle="How it works."
    sectionLede="No public repository is linked from this site. The paper is the published design. The win-loss record and the airframe mass are the portfolio record."
    links={[{ href: paper, label: "arXiv:2309.06352" }]}
>
    <Fragment slot="prose">
        <p>
            Led the software for a championship robotics team, 2023–24. The
            record on this site is 152–0. Edge inference is a quantized
            YOLOv5 model exported to ONNX, on a 150 g airframe, with
            decentralized acoustic tracking for position hold. The stack
            named here is Rust, ROS 2, YOLOv5, and ONNX.
        </p>
        <p>
            The published design is
            <a href={paper}>Lighter-Than-Air Autonomous Ball Capture and Scoring Robot</a>
            (arXiv:2309.06352). Yojan Gautam is a co-author. Yaw uses
            differential drive: motors <code>m1</code> and <code>m2</code>,
            placed 1000 mm apart. Altitude uses <code>m3</code>. Forward
            thrust uses <code>m4</code>, so yaw stays decoupled from surge.
            In search, the agent spins at full throttle on the yaw channel.
            When a target is visible, a PD controller tracks yaw error, and
            another PD tracks vertical error. The YOLOv5 and Coral perception
            pipeline is in the paper’s appendix. The competition deployment
            described there is April 2023.
        </p>
        <p>
            An educational kit for 20 students is a separate project on the
            about page. It is not this airframe.
        </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="facts">
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Years</dt>
            <dd>2023–24</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Record</dt>
            <dd>152–0, on this site</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Airframe</dt>
            <dd>150 g</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Perception</dt>
            <dd>YOLOv5, quantized to ONNX</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Paper</dt>
            <dd>arXiv:2309.06352</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Role</dt>
            <dd>Software lead, co-author</dd>
        </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="steps">
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">01</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Yaw, altitude, surge</h3>
                <p>
                    The paper’s scoring agent yaws with two motors a metre
                    apart, holds altitude with a third, and surges with a
                    fourth. Search is an open-loop spin. A visible target
                    switches yaw and altitude to PD.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">02</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Seeing the target</h3>
                <p>
                    The appendix documents a YOLOv5 pipeline on a Coral. The
                    portfolio line for the same work is that model quantized
                    to ONNX, running on the 150 g airframe, plus decentralized
                    acoustic tracking for position hold.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">03</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>What is not public here</h3>
                <p>
                    The competition code is not in a public repository linked
                    from this site. The paper is the design you can read. The
                    152–0 record and the 150 g mass are stated on this site,
                    not as a table in that paper.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
    </Fragment>
</ProjectPage>
